Creating a simple YAML parser in Go without using third-party libraries is a good exercise to understand how parsers work. Below is a basic implementation of a YAML parser that can handle simple key-value pairs and nested structures. This parser will not cover all YAML features but will give you a good starting point.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"strings"
)

type YAMLNode map[string]interface{}

func ParseYAML(input string) YAMLNode {
	lines := strings.Split(input, "\n")
	return parseYAMLLines(lines)
}

func parseYAMLLines(lines []string) YAMLNode {
	root := YAMLNode{}
	currentNode := root
	indentStack := []int{0}

	for _, line := range lines {
		trimmedLine := strings.TrimSpace(line)
		if trimmedLine == "" {
			continue
		}

		indent := getIndent(line)
		keyValue := strings.SplitN(trimmedLine, ":", 2)
		if len(keyValue) != 2 {
			fmt.Println("Invalid line:", line)
			continue
		}

		key := strings.TrimSpace(keyValue[0])
		value := strings.TrimSpace(keyValue[1])

		// Adjust the current node based on the indentation
		for indent <= indentStack[len(indentStack)-1] {
			indentStack = indentStack[:len(indentStack)-1]
			currentNode = root
			for _, i := range indentStack[1:] {
				currentNode = currentNode[strings.Repeat(" ", i)].(YAMLNode)
			}
		}
		indentStack = append(indentStack, indent)

		// Parse the value
		parsedValue := parseSimpleValue(value)
		currentNode[key] = parsedValue

		// Create a new node if the value is a map
		if parsedValue == nil {
			currentNode[key] = YAMLNode{}
			currentNode = currentNode[key].(YAMLNode)
		}
	}

	return root
}

func getIndent(s string) int {
	return len(s) - len(strings.TrimLeft(s, " "))
}

func parseSimpleValue(value string) interface{} {
	if value == "" {
		return nil
	}
	if value == "null" {
		return nil
	}
	if value == "true" {
		return true
	}
	if value == "false" {
		return false
	}
	if i, err := strconv.Atoi(value); err == nil {
		return i
	}
	if f, err := strconv.ParseFloat(value, 64); err == nil {
		return f
	}
	return value
}

func main() {
	yaml := `
name: John Doe
age: 30
address:
  street: 123 Main St
  city: Anytown
  zip: 12345
`
	node := ParseYAML(yaml)
	fmt.Printf("%+v\n", node)
}
```

This code defines a simple YAML parser that can handle basic YAML structures. It uses indentation to determine the hierarchy of the YAML structure and parses simple values like strings, integers, floats, booleans, and `null`. The `parseYAMLLines` function processes each line of the input, adjusting the current node based on the indentation level. The `parseSimpleValue` function attempts to parse the value as a number, boolean, or string.

Please note that this is a very basic implementation and does not cover all YAML features such as anchors, aliases, or more complex data types. For production use, it is recommended to use a well-tested YAML parsing library.
